<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ammi-saduqa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1646–1626 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century-bce">17th century BCE</a> · Also known as Ammisaduqa, Ammizaduga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ammi-saduqa was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-babylonian-empire">First Dynasty of Babylon</a>, who ruled in 1646–1626 BCE by the High Middle Chronology or 1638–1618 BCE by the Low Middle Chronology. He was the eldest son and successor of Ammi-ditana, probably born of Queen Šamuḫtum. Some twenty-one <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/year-name">year-names</a> survive for his reign, including the first seventeen, and they suggest fairly peaceful years in which he was chiefly occupied with enriching and enlarging the temples, as well as other building, such as a wall at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>. His son Samsu-ditana, the last king of the Amorite dynasty, succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ammi-saduqa.jpg" alt="Ammi-saduqa" width="330" height="556" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Venus_Tablet_of_Ammisaduqa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
